            <title><![CDATA[Python's experimental JIT to be removed (304 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://lwn.net/SubscriberLink/1102102/0c00e4d550f25f8c/</link>
            <guid>50257419</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:17:57 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://lwn.net/SubscriberLink/1102102/0c00e4d550f25f8c/">https://lwn.net/SubscriberLink/1102102/0c00e4d550f25f8c/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50257419">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
                	<summary>[+] Proceed to the article</summary>
                
<p>Python's <a href="https://docs.python.org/3.13/whatsnew/3.13.html#an-experimental-just-in-time-jit-compiler">experimental
just-in-time (JIT) compiler</a>, which was added to CPython in 2024 for the <a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3130/">3.13&nbsp;release</a>,
is on its way out. In June, the Python steering council <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/an-announcement-from-the-steering-council-regarding-the-jit-project/107638">announced</a>
that it was pausing work on the JIT in CPython's main branch until there was an
accepted <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001">Python Enhancement
Proposal</a> (PEP) that addressed concerns about maintenance, compatibility, and
set clear success metrics as well as a timeline for completion. After creating
<a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0836/">PEP&nbsp;836</a> ("JIT Go Brrr: The
Path to a Supported JIT Compiler for CPython")
to try to meet the council's request, and after months of discussion, the
JIT maintainers have withdrawn the PEP. For now, it appears that development on
the once-promising project has come to an end.</p>

<p>The JIT was <a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/113465">added to CPython by Brandt
Bucher</a> in early 2024. Generally, such a major feature would be expected to
land only after the council had approved a <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001/#pep-types:~:text=A%20Standards%20Track%20PEP">standards-track PEP</a>. Bucher had given
a talk (<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HxSHIpEQRjs">YouTube video</a>,
<a href="https://github.com/brandtbucher/brandtbucher/blob/master/2023/10/10/a_jit_compiler_for_cpython.pdf">slides</a>)
about the JIT at a Python core developer sprint, and <a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/113465#issuecomment-1911279536">announced
his intent</a> to merge the feature—but he had not taken the step of
authoring or submitting a PEP for its approval. He was <a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/113465#issuecomment-1969415137">prodded
into doing so</a> by steering council member Thomas Wouters, who was also
serving as the release manager for Python 3.13.</p>

<p>Bucher and Savannah Ostrowski drafted <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0744/">PEP&nbsp;744</a> ("JIT Compilation"),
which was an <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001/#pep-types:~:text=An%20Informational%20PEP%20describes%20a%20Python%20design%20issue">informational
PEP</a>—meaning it only provides information to the Python community and
does not represent formal consensus to proceed on a feature or project. LWN <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/970397/">covered</a> the discussion of
PEP&nbsp;744 in April&nbsp;2024, and later covered Bucher's <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/977855/">talk on the JIT</a> at
PyCon&nbsp;US&nbsp;2024, as well as the <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/1029307/">follow-up talk</a> at
PyCon&nbsp;US&nbsp;2025.</p>

<h4>PEP it up</h4>

<p>Work on the JIT had been proceeding on the basis of PEP&nbsp;744 until this
June, when the council had put forward its formal request for the JIT maintainers to
create a standards-track PEP. One might wonder, <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/an-announcement-from-the-steering-council-regarding-the-jit-project/107638/2">as
Antoine Pitrou did</a>, why the council had decided that it was time to speak up
and ask for a new PEP. Wouters <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/an-announcement-from-the-steering-council-regarding-the-jit-project/107638/4">said</a>
that there was no "<q>single triggering event for this request</q>", but that
the council had been discussing the topic for a while and indicated that it was
looking to ensure the JIT work did not interfere with other projects. "<q>There's been work
trying to get the JIT to play nice with instrumentation, debugging, and
profiling. There's also external JITs (like CinderX), JIT-adjacent things (like
Numba and PyTorch's compile), and discussions about a tier 3 JIT.</q>" The
council had waited until after the Python&nbsp;3.15 beta release "<q>to be clear
that we're OK with the status quo for 3.15</q>".</p>

<p>The council asked for a PEP that would make the case for the JIT "<q>as a supported,
non-experimental part of CPython: its guarantees, its maintenance commitments,
and its impact on redistributors</q>". The council set a deadline of six months
for the PEP to be submitted and accepted; failing that, the council would
require the JIT code to be removed from the CPython main branch.</p>

<!-- middle-ad -->

<p>Bucher, Ostrowski, and Ken Jin drafted PEP&nbsp;836 and <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010">published
it on the Python forum</a> for community feedback on July&nbsp;3. It generated
quite a bit of discussion, some favorable and some critical. One of the key
themes in the comments, such as <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/19">this
one</a> by Wouters, and <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/28">this
comment</a> by Mark Shannon, was that the draft felt like it should be two separate
PEPs. Shannon, for example, thought that the PEP should only
cover goals for accepting the JIT work and felt that it should leave out
implementation details. There were also quite a few calls for additional
information.</p>

<p>Bucher <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/21">pushed
back</a> a bit on the suggestion that it should be two PEPs, and Ostrowski <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/22">defended</a>
the structure of the PEP as it was. "<q>Outlining goals like we have in the PEP
without being able to share the plan and everything we've thought through
alongside it would make me uncomfortable. To me, goals without plans behind them
aren't really goals at all.</q>"</p>

<p>When we <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/1090385/">last looked at this
topic</a> in September, the discussion about the PEP had slowed down without a
commitment to develop new PEPs or a formal statement from the council. The last
word from the council at that time, in the form of a <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/2026-08-06-python-steering-council-meeting-summary/108612">summary
of its August&nbsp;6 meeting</a>, was that it had met with the PEP authors and
was "<q>discussing the state of the PEP and whether it answered the questions
the SC asked in its request for a PEP</q>".</p>

<h4>Experimentation in main</h4>

<p>On September&nbsp;21, Wouters <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/53">posted</a>
a statement from the steering council, minus Ostrowski (who is currently a
member of the steering council but did not participate since she is a co-author
of the PEP), that summarized its position. The statement
said that PEP did answer all of the questions that were highlighted in the
council's request, "<q>although perhaps some less convincingly than
others</q>".</p>

<p>The council wished to make clear that it was not
looking for the criteria that the JIT should meet to be a supported feature, or
whether it should be considered such. Instead, the council was evaluating
whether the JIT should continue as an experiment in CPython's main branch.</p>

<blockquote>
Part of the problem with allowing experimentation in main is the lack of unified
design, and the uncertainty that core developers, package maintainers and users
face as the experimentation changes behaviours and API surfaces.
</blockquote>

<p>The council said that the PEP was not specific enough about support plans, performance
criteria, and too vague or too optimistic about a number of other areas. The
statement included a ten-point list of items that the council wanted "<q>to see
either spelled out in the PEP, or at least discussed and rejected</q>". The
council's list expressed concerns ranging from memory impact to security. For
example, on the topic of platform parity, the council wanted to ensure that the
JIT was designed to be easily adopted for new platforms. It wanted assurance
that the JIT's design would not be tied to specific platform or require
platform-specific knowledge to maintain.</p>

<p>Another area that the council called out was user impact. It said that the
PEP was "<q>overly optimistic</q>" about user-visible differences with the JIT
enabled. There were many cases where users depend on idiosyncrasies in the
interpreter design, "<q>as we've seen with other Python implementations or even
just between major Python versions</q>", and it wanted clarification on how
those differences would be handled. Would they be considered bugs, how would
situations be handled if a difference in behavior was unavoidable, and so
on.</p>

<p>Security was "<q>an underserved area in the PEP</q>", according to the
council; it suggested that a discussion with the <a href="https://devguide.python.org/security/psrt/">Python security response
team</a> was in order and that a security review should be part of the criteria
for supported status as well.</p>

<p>PEP 836 specified a <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0836/#compatibility-review">compatibility
review</a> milestone to correspond with the first Python&nbsp;3.17 release
candidate—which would be expected sometime in August&nbsp;2028—that
would involve running the test suites of the top PyPI packages to see if the JIT
broke them. The council wanted that timeline moved up significantly. "<q>We
think that's way, way too late, and the compatibility review should be an
ongoing and continuous process starting as soon as possible</q>".

</p><p>The council also worried about the JIT's design decisions being made
"<q>without critical review by the larger group of developers ultimately
responsible for [CPython] development, maintenance and support</q>". The bottom
line, it said, was that PEP&nbsp;836 "<q>mostly answers the questions we
asked</q>" with no missing pieces, but it wanted more discussion on those topics
so it could "<q>gauge community support and consensus before we're asked to
pronounce on this PEP</q>".</p>

<p>Jin <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/55">replied</a>
a few days later to thank the council for its detailed feedback and said that the
group would work on its suggestions.</p>

<h4>Withdrawn</h4>

<p>After some consideration, it appears, the PEP's authors have decided not to
continue pursing the council's approval. On October&nbsp;19, Ostrowski (who is
also release manager for Python&nbsp;3.16 and 3.17) <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/57">announced</a>
that the PEP was being withdrawn:</p>

<blockquote>
The Steering Council rightly sets a high bar for a proposal of this scope, and
we don't feel we have the personal bandwidth to carry the current version
forward. We continue to believe in the technical direction outlined in the PEP,
including free-threaded support and a method-based frontend, and remain
optimistic about the JIT's potential in CPython. We'll make the reference
implementation available and link it from the withdrawn PEP so others who are
interested can explore and build on that.
</blockquote>

<p>Barry Scott <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/59">asked</a>
if that meant that work on the JIT was stopped. Brett Cannon <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/61">replied</a>
that the work would be stopped in the main repository, but it would be up to the
community whether the work would continue elsewhere. Dunc Graham <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/62">suggested</a>
that there might be time for an alternative PEP to be submitted before the
council's deadline. Cannon <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/63">said</a>
that was not likely, "<q>as all the people actively driving the JIT were either
authors on that PEP or contributors to it</q>".</p>

<p>With the PEP withdrawn, the JIT will likely be removed from CPython main
fairly soon to ensure that any problems caused by its removal can be addressed
early in the <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0826/">Python&nbsp;3.16
development cycle</a>. It is unclear whether the original drivers of the JIT
work intend to continue working on it at all, or if it will simply be left for others to
pick up if there is enough community interest. I have emailed the PEP authors
for clarification, but have not yet received a reply.</p><br clear="all">
               <br clear="all">
               <blockquote>
