        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[The Mythical Man-Month in the Age of AI (362 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://j11g.com/the-mythical-man-month-in-the-age-of-ai</link>
            <guid>50255298</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 06:25:37 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://j11g.com/the-mythical-man-month-in-the-age-of-ai">https://j11g.com/the-mythical-man-month-in-the-age-of-ai</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50255298">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
                                            <article>
                
                                    <p>
                                                     <time datetime="2026-10-27T07:14:00+01:00">27 October 2026</time>
                                                                    </p>
                
                <p>Last weekend I watched four people desperately trying to get a laptop to connect to a screen. I was happily observing their efforts, with zero intent to join in.</p>
<p>But someone nudged me: can't <em>you</em> have a look at it?</p>
<p>My reply: "Haha sorry no! My experience is that adding more people will not solve this problem any quicker".</p>
<p>I was of course -- indirectly -- referring to Brooks's Law:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Adding manpower to a late software project makes it later."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It was formulated by computer scientist Frederick P. Brooks Jr. in his 1975 book The Mythical Man-Month, based on his experience managing the IBM OS/360 project.</p>
<p>However I got to thinking. Does this still hold up in the age of AI agents and LLMs?</p>
<p>At first glance, you’d think no. The traditional bottlenecks -- onboarding time, endless stand-ups, and office politics -- disappear overnight. An AI agent is up to speed in milliseconds and doesn't demand a coffee break.</p>
<p>But when you look closer, and the law hasn't vanished. It just mutated.</p>
<p>The famous <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooks%27s_law#:~:text=Adding%20more%20people,in%20one%20month%22." rel="noopener noreferrer">"Baby Problem"</a> remains: nine agents still can’t deliver a baby in one month. Sequential tasks are still sequential.</p>
<p>Overhead hasn't disappeared; it shifted: instead of managing people, you are now managing agents. Someone still needs to define "done", review the code, catch hallucinations, and stitch the pieces together. </p>
<p>That someone is you.</p>
<p>Throw nine autonomous agents at a single codebase without crystal-clear orchestration, and they will happily rewrite each other's work in a high-speed loop.</p>
<p>Maybe the 2026 update to Brooks’s Law should be:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Adding agents to a project with unclear requirements gets you the wrong answer faster."</p>
</blockquote>                





            </article>
                                </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
