        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Microsoft offers voluntary buyouts to a quarter of its workforce (130 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.kuow.org/technology/2026-10-26/microsoft-offers-voluntary-buyouts-to-a-quarter-of-its-workforce</link>
            <guid>50250937</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:05:15 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.kuow.org/technology/2026-10-26/microsoft-offers-voluntary-buyouts-to-a-quarter-of-its-workforce">https://www.kuow.org/technology/2026-10-26/microsoft-offers-voluntary-buyouts-to-a-quarter-of-its-workforce</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50250937">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p>
        Published&nbsp;October 26, 2026 at 10:16 AM PDT
    </p>
    <meta content="2026-10-26T17:16:19.448Z">


                                        </div><div>
                                        <p>Microsoft is offering voluntary buyouts to about 60,000 employees in an effort to further reduce its headcount.</p><p>Chief People Officer Amy Coleman <a href="https://blogs.microsoft.com/blog/2026/10/26/introducing-a-voluntary-exit-offer/" target="_blank">sent a memo</a> to employees Monday detailing the terms of the voluntary exit offer, which will be available to roughly one quarter of Microsoft’s workforce.</p><p>“This offer gives eligible employees who may be considering a future outside the company a new choice to make that transition now with support,” Hood said in the memo. “It also gives participating organizations another way to adapt their workforce to changing needs.”</p>

<p>The buyouts will be available to select employees in the U.S., U.K., Canada, and India at level 70 and below in the company’s ranking system. That ceiling applies to the vice president level at the company.</p><p>A Microsoft spokesperson said the offer will be comparable to the company’s standard severance package, which varies by market but typically includes base pay for a certain amount of time (based on tenure), stock options granted over time, health care, and career planning services.</p><p>The exit offer comes a few months after Microsoft offered voluntary early retirement to employees of a certain tenure at level 67 and below. About 30% of eligible employees took that offer, though Microsoft expects this broader round of buyouts to have a smaller adoption rate. That’s, in part, because the voluntary retirement program offered more generous terms to incentivize uptake.</p><p>Eligibility for the new program was determined by the heads of each organization at Microsoft. That means the sales organization, some corporate services like marketing, and some engineering teams will not be participating.</p><p>The spokesperson said artificial intelligence is not driving the buyout programs. Microsoft has, however, been on an employee-cutting spree for several years as it invests billions of dollars into the infrastructure needed to train and run AI models.</p><p>“The Voluntary Exit Offer is new for us. … There is no one-size-fits-all approach for every team or every employee,” Hood said in the memo. “These are a few of the ways we’re trying to meet different business needs while continuing to create opportunities for our own people to grow and contribute.”</p>
                                    </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Somebody please stop the spam texts (143 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://notes.npilk.com/stop-the-spam-texts</link>
            <guid>50250016</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:57:32 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://notes.npilk.com/stop-the-spam-texts">https://notes.npilk.com/stop-the-spam-texts</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50250016">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>

    

    

    <p><strong>Summary:</strong> I complain about spam texts and consider why I’m so hesitant to just reply STOP to unsubscribe.</p>

    <p>I moved out of Texas a few years ago. Unfortunately, it doesn’t seem like any statewide midterm candidates have heard about that. In the past month, I’ve gotten dozens of texts like this, usually multiple per day:</p>

    <div>
    <p><img src="https://notes.npilk.com/resources/stop-the-spam-texts/texts.jpeg" alt="Three political text messages: a voting reminder from OurBallot, a Don Huffines campaign ad, and a Clear Insights survey request"></p><p><em>Some of the texts I received while working on this post</em></p>
    </div>

    <p>They seem to be mostly from Republicans, but I’ve gotten a little bit of everything — texts from Democrats, independents, PACs, unbiased pollsters, obviously biased pollsters, even those weird <a href="https://www.fox4news.com/news/political-mailers-transgender-resources-children-north-texas">false flag texts</a> that went around. I don’t remember signing up for anything, and certainly would never have intended to, but clearly my number got on a list somehow.</p>

    <p>This is pretty annoying, because text is my highest-signal communication channel. Most of what’s in there is from family and friends, plus the occasional 2FA code. The little red notification bubble means a lot more on Messages than other apps — which of course is exactly why politicians, marketers, and scammers want so badly to get in there.</p>

    <p>It’s not just the elections, either — this has been getting noticeably worse over time. This year I’ve started to get a ton of these types of messages, sometimes as many as one a day:</p>

    <p><img src="https://notes.npilk.com/resources/stop-the-spam-texts/text2.jpeg" alt="Text message from 'Amy from TN Tree Service' offering a free estimate for tree trimming">
    </p>

    <p>It turns out, despite seeming all personal and handwritten, that if you reply STOP, these messages also reveal themselves to be part of a larger campaign. (Shouldn’t this be illegal, by the way?)</p>

    <p><img src="https://notes.npilk.com/resources/stop-the-spam-texts/text_stop.jpeg" alt="Replying STOP to the tree service text returns an automated opt-out confirmation">
    </p>

    <p>So it seems like the solution is to reply STOP to all of these and eventually you won’t get as many, right?</p>

    <p>Except I haven’t done that. I don’t want to do that. Except for that “experiment” above, I have instead chosen “Delete and Report Spam” for every single one of these texts… even though it doesn’t seem to do anything.</p>

    <p>What’s crazy to me is that Apple has actually added a Spam folder to Messages, and there are a few things in there. But even though I’ve been reporting all this stuff as spam for months, if not longer, none of the new messages I get seem to get filtered into the Spam folder.</p>

    <p>So again, why not just reply STOP to these messages? Why am I so averse to doing that?</p>

    <p>After some reflection, I think I figured it out: <strong>replying with STOP feels like I’m accepting this vision of the world.</strong></p>

    <p>If I reply STOP, I’m basically saying, “Okay, you’re allowed to send me whatever you want, and then I have to send back STOP for you to knock it off.” And I want to fundamentally reject that framing. Mass-texting and spam campaigns shouldn’t get that right by default, and the burden shouldn’t be on me to individually opt-out of every single one of these campaigns.</p>

    <p>So if you’re reading this and you work on iOS at Apple, <em>please help</em>. This feels like a great use case for all this new Apple Intelligence (similar to the <a href="https://notes.npilk.com/experiments-with-ai-adblock">AI adblock experiment</a> I ran a while back). If I report something as Spam, maybe an option could pop up to “Filter messages like this to Spam?” or something, and then the unwanted text gets added to a list of examples. So then next time I get a text from an unknown number, the LLM reads the list of spam examples, and if the new message is similar, it goes to Spam. (That’s just off the top of my head; I’m sure there are even better ideas to combat this.)</p>

    <p>Recently, I did find a “Delete and Stop” option, which auto-sends the STOP message for me. But it seems like “Delete and Stop” and “Delete and Report Spam” are mutually exclusive. You’d think that reporting as spam would also auto-send a STOP message, but the number of texts I’ve gotten from Don Huffines despite reporting each one suggests otherwise.</p>

    <p>Hopefully things will calm down after the election. But for goodness’ sake, somebody please do something about this. I was going to say we fixed this problem for email, but I’m not really sure we did — these days I get tons of marketing messages despite always trying to opt out on every single website whenever I purchase something. And I get multiple spam calls a day too, even though I’m on the Do Not Call registry. So maybe we’re just doomed to be eternally pestered by whoever wants our money or our vote. Here’s hoping they don’t figure out how to get into my Slack. <span>⧈</span></p>

    <hr>

    

    </div></div>]]></description>
