            <title><![CDATA[Oldest GUI application that still runs on Windows 11 (453 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://blog.enlyth.com/posts/oldest-windows-gui-app-win-11/</link>
            <guid>50248055</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:56:55 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://blog.enlyth.com/posts/oldest-windows-gui-app-win-11/">https://blog.enlyth.com/posts/oldest-windows-gui-app-win-11/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50248055">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-astro-cid-vt34qafq=""><h2 id="early-investigation">Early investigation</h2>
<p>For a while I’ve been wondering about a silly question, which is, what’s the oldest application with a GUI that will still run on Windows 11 without modifications or emulation.</p>
<p>Given Windows’ excellent backwards compatibility, I expected the answer to be something impressively old.</p>
<p>Sadly, Windows 11 can’t run 16-bit Windows programs by itself, because 64-bit Windows has no <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_DOS_machine#Windows_NTVDM">NTVDM</a>, so we’re going to have to find something from the early NT era.</p>
<p>I remember asking this question a while ago on an anonymous forum, where someone pointed me to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Win32s">Win32s</a>, and suggested to find an early release version of it with <code>FREECELL.exe</code>.</p>
<p>So I did exactly that, and lo and behold, <code>FREECELL.EXE</code> with a date modified of <code>22-Feb-93</code> running and fully playable, without any changes on the latest version of Windows 11:</p>
<p><img src="https://blog.enlyth.com/_astro/image.Bg57xYSH_1uh08L.webp" alt="FREECELL.EXE from 1993" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="638" height="431"></p>
<p>At the time of this post, that’s a 33 years and 8 months old application, which is impressively almost as old as me.</p>
<p>So I kinda thought “cool”, considered this a solved mystery and forgot about it shortly afterwards. But for some reason, this question popped into my mind again lately and this time I decided to dig deeper to see if I can find something older.</p>
<h2 id="further-efforts">Further efforts</h2>
<p>Digging through old Windows versions on the Internet Archive, I found some early NT 3.1 pre-release versions that seemed promising, but the main issue seemed to be either incompatibility with today’s executable format or API changes that came later in development.</p>
<p>The earliest 1991 versions used a draft PE format not compatible with modern Windows, and builds up to mid-1992 assumed the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X86_calling_conventions#cdecl"><code>cdecl</code></a> calling convention instead of <code>stdcall</code> for Windows API calls, so arguments would get removed twice, as such:</p>
<pre tabindex="0" data-language="plaintext"><code><span><span>push hwnd          ; 4 bytes of args</span></span>
<span><span>call [ShowWindow]  ; Win11 ShowWindow is stdcall, ends with "ret 4" and removes them</span></span>
<span><span>add  esp, 4        ; the 1992 caller removes them again</span></span></code></pre>
<p>Which would understandably cause the program to crash.</p>
<p>There was one application that caught my eye though, from Build 347, and that was <code>CDPLAYER.EXE</code>, linked on <code>16-Nov-1992</code>. By then the calling convention was fixed, but the other programs in that build crash in their bundled C runtime. <code>CDPLAYER.EXE</code> avoids that by using the system’s <code>crtdll.dll</code>, and Windows 11 still ships a <code>crtdll.dll</code> in <code>SysWOW64</code>.</p>
<p>If you start the program, you get something like this:</p>
<p><img src="https://blog.enlyth.com/_astro/image-3.ccUj1wn8_1qfbsq.webp" alt="CDPLAYER.EXE no CD-ROM drive found" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="283" height="254"></p>
<p>That is because, duh, my computer doesn’t have a CD-ROM drive. But you can use a small DLL (<code>cdemu.dll</code>) to emulate one with some fake audio data and actually get it to play audio:</p>
<p><img src="https://blog.enlyth.com/_astro/image-2.CW-t_DpM_vg2wv.webp" alt="CDPLAYER.EXE with emulated CD-ROM" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="312" height="254"></p>
<p>Pretty cool for a program that’s almost 34 years old!</p>
<h2 id="what-if-we-cheat">What if we cheat?</h2>
<p>So earlier I mentioned that 16-bit programs are out of the picture because they are no longer supported.</p>
<p>But if you want, you can use something like <a href="https://github.com/otya128/winevdm">otvdm</a> to run older Windows applications.</p>
<p>Behold, a fully playable version of <code>REVERSI.EXE</code> dated <code>15-Nov-1985</code> running on Windows 11, taken from Windows 1.01:</p>
<p><img src="https://blog.enlyth.com/_astro/image-4.BUOY9wBP_ZSDHhX.webp" alt="alt text" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="561" height="453"></p>
<p>And although using <code>otvdm</code> does feel like cheating, I thought it was cool enough to include in this post.</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[In Leaked Audio, Flock CEO Tells Cops Backlash to the Company Isn't Real (299 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.404media.co/in-leaked-audio-flock-ceo-tells-cops-backlash-to-the-company-isnt-real/</link>
            <guid>50248005</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:54:00 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.404media.co/in-leaked-audio-flock-ceo-tells-cops-backlash-to-the-company-isnt-real/">https://www.404media.co/in-leaked-audio-flock-ceo-tells-cops-backlash-to-the-company-isnt-real/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50248005">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
        <article>
          <div>
              
<!--kg-card-begin: html-->

<!--kg-card-end: html-->
<p>Garrett Langley, the CEO of automatic license plate reader company Flock, told an audience of police chiefs at a private police conference Sunday that anti-Flock protests around the country are not real, according to leaked audio obtained by 404 Media.</p><p>He also said his wife advised him to never apologize, said he would prefer to never do interviews with the press again, and claimed without evidence that the social media opposition to Flock is driven mostly by Russian bots.</p><p>“Somebody was paying for [the social media posts and] stopped paying for it,” Langley said about the many viral posts criticizing his company’s technology. “Social media chatter on Flock is down about 95 percent from July. It went down 50 percent last week when Ukraine bombed a Russian data center with a drone. Literally 50 percent overnight. And so look, I’m the last person, my wife as well, she’s like ‘This is crazy, these people are getting paid to say these things,’” he added, <a href="https://www.404media.co/data-centers-are-war-targets-now/"><u>repeating a disproven theory</u></a> that Ukraine’s strikes on Russian data centers had a significant impact on internet bot activity.&nbsp;</p><!--members-only--><p>“It’s anonymous bots, and so it is not real,” he continued. “Inorganic activity has led to organic activity, but when I look at protests across the country, it is the same people with the same signs and the same message. That’s not normal. That’s not organic at all.” Langley’s comments echo another unsubstantiated theory that said opposition to Flock comes from a far-left network <a href="https://www.404media.co/smear-campaign-says-anti-flock-movement-is-chinese-propaganda/"><u>spreading propaganda at the behest of China</u></a>.</p><p>Langley made the comments at the <a href="https://www.theiacp.org/?ref=404media.co"><u>International Association of Chiefs of Police</u></a> (IACP) conference, which is <a href="https://www.theiacpconference.org/?ref=404media.co"><u>running Saturday to Tuesday</u></a> in Orlando, Florida. Many law enforcement contractors exhibit at the conference, and speakers get to attend with more <a href="https://www.theiacpconference.org/about-the-conference/?ref=404media.co#site-navigation"><u>than 16,000 public safety officials</u></a>.</p><figure data-kg-thumbnail="https://storage.ghost.io/c/0f/76/0f76b548-bc58-4f25-abc3-3f5ebca07da4/content/media/2026/10/In-Leaked-Audio--Flock-CEO-Tells-Cops-Backlash-to-Company-is----Paid-For-----3-_thumb.jpg" data-kg-custom-thumbnail="">
            <div>
                <video src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/0f/76/0f76b548-bc58-4f25-abc3-3f5ebca07da4/content/media/2026/10/In-Leaked-Audio--Flock-CEO-Tells-Cops-Backlash-to-Company-is----Paid-For-----3-.mp4" poster="data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7" width="1080" height="1920" playsinline="" preload="metadata"></video>
                
                <div>
                        <p>
                        
                        <span>0:00</span></p><p>
                            /<span>1:39</span>
                        </p>
                        </div>
            </div>
            
        <img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/0f/76/0f76b548-bc58-4f25-abc3-3f5ebca07da4/content/media/2026/10/In-Leaked-Audio--Flock-CEO-Tells-Cops-Backlash-to-Company-is----Paid-For-----3-_thumb.jpg"></figure><p>Flock makes automatic license plate readers (ALPRs) and other surveillance cameras. Its ALPRs constantly scan the license plate, color, make, and model of every vehicle that drives past, creating a timestamped log of peoples’ movements. The embattled company has faced extensive media and public scrutiny as instances of police abuse, false arrests, and the use of the technology for immigration enforcement have come to light. Dozens of cities have <a href="https://www.404media.co/cities-are-ditching-flock-immediately-replacing-it-with-axon-license-plate-readers/" rel="noreferrer">canceled their contracts</a> with Flock over the last 18 months.&nbsp;</p><p>As backlash to the company grew, Langley began doing damage control with the media, which <a href="https://www.forbes.com/sites/thomasbrewster/2026/07/17/flock-ceo-sorry-for-labelling-activists-terrorists/?ref=404media.co"><u>included apologizing</u></a> for calling DeFlock, a group that maps the location of Flock cameras, a “<a href="https://www.forbes.com/sites/thomasbrewster/2025/09/03/ai-startup-flock-thinks-it-can-eliminate-all-crime-in-america/?ref=404media.co"><u>terroristic organization</u></a>.” In the audio from the IACP conference, Langley suggested that he generally should not apologize for the company.</p><div><p>💡</p><p><b><strong>Do you know anything else about Flock? We would love to hear from you. Using a non-work device, you can message Joseph securely on Signal at joseph.404, or Jason on jason.404</strong></b></p></div><p>“I give my wife a lot of credit. She was, like, you should never apologize,” Langley said. “Sure, people make mistakes. I've made a lot of mistakes in the last nine years. I made a lot of mistakes this summer. But we do good work.”</p><p>The session itself was framed as a fireside chat between Langley and David Rausch, IACP’s president and director of the Tennessee Bureau of Investigation. The schedule said the session would “examine the opportunities, challenges, and public debate surrounding the technology [ALPRs], including issues related to privacy, transparency, governance, data use, and community trust.”&nbsp;</p><div><p><img src="" alt="audio-thumbnail"></p></div><p>Flock makes the ALPR data available to its law enforcement customers, who can query it without a warrant. Flock differentiates itself from other ALPR providers, like Axon, by providing a national network of ALPRs. A cop in one part of the U.S. can search cameras not just in their own jurisdiction, or even state, but all over the country, if those other police departments have added their cameras to the network. A <a href="https://www.404media.co/cbp-had-access-to-more-than-80-000-flock-ai-cameras-nationwide/"><u>recent count</u></a> put the national network at around 80,000 cameras.</p><p>That national network is a large part of why Flock has faced so much criticism. <a href="https://www.404media.co/ice-taps-into-nationwide-ai-enabled-camera-network-data-shows/"><u>404 Media revealed last year</u></a> that local cops were doing lookups in Flock for Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE), and a cop in Texas <a href="https://www.404media.co/a-texas-cop-searched-license-plate-cameras-nationwide-for-a-woman-who-got-an-abortion/"><u>searched cameras nationwide</u></a> for a woman who self-administered an abortion.</p><p>Langley said he’d like to not do any more media interviews. “The media gets to say whatever they want to say about you,” he said.&nbsp;</p><p>“I’d like to never do media again if that's possible. I don’t know if any chiefs can tell me. I’m going to try to figure this out but I think for me, I wish we could do this more often and you know I’m excited about the work we’re doing together and think we have a lot of ideas to continue to make up,” Langley said.&nbsp;</p><p>404 Media’s investigations on Flock and others have triggered a nationwide conversation about whether individual communities want Flock cameras stationed on their roads or <a href="https://www.404media.co/city-learns-flock-accessed-cameras-in-childrens-gymnastics-room-as-a-sales-pitch-demo-renews-contract-anyway/"><u>inside their children' s gymnastics class</u></a>. Flock is basically a meme on TikTok and Instagram now, with many creators trying to tell others about what the cameras do and the issues they raise (with varying accuracy, and some people making <a href="https://www.404media.co/the-rise-of-anti-flock-influencers-who-make-things-up-for-clout/"><u>videos purely for the grift</u></a>). Some people have even taken to vandalizing, destroying, <a href="https://www.404media.co/hackers-stole-flocks-camera-software-revealing-how-the-company-tracks-cars-and-people-2/"><u>or reverse-engineering Flock cameras</u></a>, with <a href="https://www.404media.co/the-government-is-monitoring-anti-flock-tiktok-and-instagram-accounts/"><u>the government monitoring</u></a> anti-Flock social media accounts. That nationwide conversation has now reached the Hill, with <a href="https://www.404media.co/lawmakers-introduce-multiple-laws-to-curb-flock-after-404-media-coverage/"><u>multiple lawmakers proposing legislation</u></a> that would ban or otherwise dramatically rein in Flock.</p><p>Flock competitor Axon threw a party at the Hard Rock Cafe on Sunday night, according to the conference schedule.</p><p>Flock did not respond to a request for comment.</p><p>Langley suggested during the session with Rausch that eventually the opposition to Flock will die down: “Whenever this industry finds a highly effective tool, it has to go through this period of intense debate. But then you get on the other side. I think right now we’re not at the bottom, but we’re real, dang, near close to it and about to come back up on the other side.”</p>
<!--kg-card-begin: html-->

<!--kg-card-end: html-->

                    <div>
    <div>
      <p>About the author</p>
      <p>Joseph is an award-winning investigative journalist focused on generating impact. His work has triggered hundreds of millions of dollars worth of fines, shut down tech companies, and much more.</p>
      
    </div>
      <p><img data-src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/0f/76/0f76b548-bc58-4f25-abc3-3f5ebca07da4/content/images/2023/08/404-joseph-01-1.jpg" alt="Joseph Cox" src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/0f/76/0f76b548-bc58-4f25-abc3-3f5ebca07da4/content/images/2023/08/404-joseph-01-1.jpg">  
      </p>
  </div>
                    <div>
    <div>
      <p>About the author</p>
      <p>Jason is a cofounder of 404 Media. He was previously the editor-in-chief of Motherboard. He loves the Freedom of Information Act and surfing.</p>
      
    </div>
      <p><img data-src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/0f/76/0f76b548-bc58-4f25-abc3-3f5ebca07da4/content/images/2023/08/404-jason-01-copy.jpeg" alt="Jason Koebler" src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/0f/76/0f76b548-bc58-4f25-abc3-3f5ebca07da4/content/images/2023/08/404-jason-01-copy.jpeg">  
      </p>
  </div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div></div>]]></description>
